  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 248 PM MDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nWhile latest models continue to point to significant precipitation \nacross Rockies through the middle of the week, the details on location \nof the heaviest precipitation continue to fluctuate, as models differ \non the location of reminents of subtropical system Polo as it interacts \nwith the broad West Coast trough translating across the southern Great \nBasin Monday night and Tuesday. At any rate, showers continue to spread\neast across the plains Monday night through Tuesday morning, as \nprecipitation picks up in intensity again across the Continental Divide\nwith increasing difluence aloft with a developing strong upper low across\nthe Desert Southwest. \n\nThe latest EC solution remains the strongest and furthest north with said\nupper low, which spreads widespread precipitation across south central \nand southeast Colorado Tuesday night through Wednesday night, as the system\ntracks across Northern New Mexico into the Oklahoma Panhandle, with \nprecipitation winding down from west to east through the day Thursday, as \ndrier works into the region from the north. The latest Canadian solution\nis the furthest south with the system and driest, though does bring the \npotential for heavy rainfall across the southern tier zones, while the \nGFS solution is now in between the other solutions, and is much wetter \nacross southern Colorado than its previous solutions. \n \nThe latest NBM and WPC guidance has leaned wetter across southern Colorado\nand does have 1 to 3 inches of QPF across south central and southeast \nColorado from Monday through Thursday, with greatest amounts across\nthe higher terrain and southeast plains along and south of the Highway\n50 Corridor. With that said, and if the current forecast remains true,\nlater shifts will likely need to spread Flood Watches across much of \nsouthern Colorado through this period, however, would like to see better\nmodel consistency before issuing more highlights at this time.  With the \nPacific origin of this system, snow levels will remain rather high, \ngenerally aoa 12,000 feet through out the period, with the potential for\n2 to 6 inches of snow across the highest peaks of the southern mountains.   \nHigh temperatures to remain well below seasonal levels as well through\nThursday. \n\nBy Friday and into the weekend, warm and drier air within more westerly flow \nworks back into the region, as upper level ridging builds back across the \nGreat Basin and into the Rockies. This leads to a slow warming and drying trend, \nas there will be enough residual moisture to support more isolated afternoon \nconvection.",
